Basic Screenshot Questions
	1. How do you take a screenshot in Selenium using Java?
File screenshot = ((TakesScreenshot) driver).getScreenshotAs(OutputType.FILE);
FileUtils.copyFile(screenshot, new File("screenshot.png"));
	2. Which interface is used to capture screenshots in Selenium?
		○ TakesScreenshot interface.
	3. What are the different OutputType options available for screenshots?
		○ OutputType.FILE
		○ OutputType.BYTES
		○ OutputType.BASE64
	4. Can Selenium take a screenshot of a specific element?
		○ Yes, if you're using Selenium 4+:
WebElement element = driver.findElement(By.id("elementId"));
File screenshot = element.getScreenshotAs(OutputType.FILE);
FileUtils.copyFile(screenshot, new File("element.png"));
 Intermediate/Scenario-Based Questions
	1. How do you take a screenshot when a test fails automatically?
		○ Integrate with a test framework like TestNG or JUnit, using listeners:
@AfterMethod
public void tearDown(ITestResult result) {
    if (ITestResult.FAILURE == result.getStatus()) {
        File screenshot = ((TakesScreenshot) driver).getScreenshotAs(OutputType.FILE);
        FileUtils.copyFile(screenshot, new File("failed_" + result.getName() + ".png"));
    }
}
	2. How do you capture screenshots in headless mode?
		○ Works the same way as normal mode, as long as you're using Chrome/Firefox in headless:
ChromeOptions options = new ChromeOptions();
options.addArguments("--headless");
WebDriver driver = new ChromeDriver(options);
Advanced/Tricky Questions
	1. How do you embed a screenshot into a report (like ExtentReports or Allure)?
		○ For ExtentReports:
test.log(Status.FAIL, "Screenshot attached: " + test.addScreenCaptureFromPath("screenshot.png"));
	2. How do you take full-page screenshots (not just the visible viewport)?
		○ Selenium 4 supports full-page screenshots in Firefox:
FirefoxDriver driver = new FirefoxDriver();
File screenshot = ((FirefoxDriver) driver).getFullPageScreenshotAs(OutputType.FILE);
	3. Can you capture screenshots in parallel tests?
		○ Yes, but ensure each screenshot file has a unique name (e.g., timestamp + thread ID).
	4. How do you save screenshots with timestamped filenames?
String timestamp = new SimpleDateFormat("yyyyMMddHHmmss").format(new Date());
File screenshot = ((TakesScreenshot) driver).getScreenshotAs(OutputType.FILE);
FileUtils.copyFile(screenshot, new File("screenshot_" + timestamp + ".png"));
Common Pitfalls
	• ClassCastException: Forgetting to cast the driver to TakesScreenshot.
	• FileNotFoundException: Trying to write to a path that doesn’t exist.
	• Screenshot taken too early: Take after waits or page load.


